{
  "abstract": "DICTO's paper \"From Dehn Packets to Scutoids: How the Fullerene Star Fills Space\" (Zenodo, 2026) proves, as its Theorem 4, that two pairs of icosahedral pieces whose Dehn invariants cancel cannot tile space: (a) no tiling of 3-space contains a dodecahedron centre pyramid or a truncated icosahedron if every tile is a similar copy (motion, reflection, positive scaling) of one of these two, of the dodecahedron, the icosahedron, the truncated octahedron or the truncated cuboctahedron, or a convex polytope whose dihedral angles are all nonnegative rational multiples of pi; (b) the same for the icosahedron centre pyramid and the truncated dodecahedron. The tiling need not be face to face. This entry proves (a) and (b) in Lean, with each named solid given by exact face inequalities over Q(sqrt 5) or Q(sqrt 2) and its dihedral angles read from the set itself, as in the registered Dehn Canon entry: at an edge point, the share of a small disc across the edge that lies in the tangent cone. It also proves the paper's two lemmas: Lemma 3, that pi, arctan(phi), arccos(1/sqrt 6) and arccos(sqrt(2/3)) are linearly independent over Q (Niven's theorem in Mathlib and a field argument), and Lemma 2 in the weaker form the proof of Theorem 4 needs: at an edge point x of a tile of a tiling by convex polytopes (locally finite, not necessarily face to face) there are finitely many tiles with numbers summing to 2 pi, the given tile's being its angle at x and each other being pi, 2 pi, or a dihedral angle of that tile read at some edge point of it, chosen independently and not necessarily on the given edge. The paper takes Lemma 2 from DICTO's earlier paper \"Aristotle's Pyramid\"; it is the edge-counting step behind Debrunner's theorem (1980). The proof of Theorem 4 counts the angles round one edge: the coefficients of the three irrational angles must cancel within one turn, and a weight argument removes the pieces one after another. All the paper's helpers are covered, the truncated icosidodecahedron included, each built from exact coordinates. Not formalized in the Challenge: Theorem 4 (c), on the smallest ring, is proved in the library but is not in the Challenge; the rest of the paper (scutoid fillings, the Fullerene Star). Audience: researchers on tilings, space-filling polyhedra and scissors congruence, and Mathlib contributors.",
